(How do I keep groceries from going to waste?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Reuse ingredients across meals, buy only vegetables your household can realistically) Tj
T*
(use, freeze extra bread or cooked chicken when appropriate, and plan a leftover night.) Tj
T*
(Quantities are a guide, not a requirement.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Is there a free printable?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Yes. Use the print button above to print this page or save it as a PDF. The) Tj
T*
(quick-reference grid and grocery checklist are included; browser print settings can help) Tj
T*
(you choose pages and paper size.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(How do I encourage trying new foods without a dinner battle?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Offer new foods without pressure, keep a familiar option available when practical, and) Tj
T*
(let people explore at their own pace. The aim is a calmer routine, not a clean plate or) Tj
T*
(a guaranteed bite.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Make the plan yours) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Start with the dinners your household is most likely to accept. Put the swap column to) Tj
T*
(work before anyone is hungry, cross off what you already own, and repeat your winners) Tj
T*
(next week. If all seven nights don't go to plan, that doesn't mean the plan failed. It) Tj
T*
(means you have useful information for the next grocery run.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Keep the dinner momentum going with) Tj
T*
(20 Lazy Dinners for When Your Brain Has Left the Building) Tj
T*
(or) Tj
T*
(22 Cozy Fall Dinners That Taste Like Wearing Your Favorite Sweater) Tj
T*
(.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Food safety: follow package directions, refrigerate perishable leftovers promptly, and) Tj
T*
(use reliable food-safety guidance for storage and cooking temperatures.) Tj
T*
